WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.130
I asked my class how a network with a

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:05.510
million weights knows which ones to

3
00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:05.770
change.

4
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.200
One student said: the framework does it.

5
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.190
That answer is true.

6
00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.570
It is also the moment understanding stops.

7
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.480
Today: how backpropagation computes every

8
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.600
gradient in one backward pass, and how to

9
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.200
make it concrete for students.

10
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.460
A network is a chain of simple operations.

11
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.430
The forward pass computes each

12
00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.630
value from inputs to loss.

13
00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.330
Backpropagation walks the same

14
00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.510
graph in reverse, applying the

15
00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.870
chain rule at each node.

16
00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.470
If the loss depends on a, and a depends

17
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.610
on w, then the derivative of the loss

18
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.350
with respect to w is the derivative

19
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.210
with respect to a times the derivative

20
00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.130
of a with respect to w.

21
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.240
Local derivatives multiply.

22
00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.050
With sigmoid units each local

23
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.290
derivative is at most a quarter.

24
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.630
Multiply ten of them and the gradient

25
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.350
reaching the first layer is almost zero.

26
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.020
That is why deep sigmoid

27
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.780
networks learned so slowly.

28
00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.110
Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams showed in

29
00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.430
nineteen eighty six that one backward

30
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.330
pass gives every gradient for about the

31
00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.230
cost of one forward pass.

32
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.960
Without that, training large

33
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.520
networks would be impossible.

34
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.030
Cognitive load theory says

35
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.230
novices drown in symbols.

36
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.260
So start with a graph of three

37
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.940
nodes and real numbers.

38
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.030
Students compute the forward pass,

39
00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.110
then the backward pass, on paper.

40
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.560
Only then generalise.

41
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:49.290
Quick check!

42
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.870
The derivative of the loss with

43
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.510
respect to a is two, and the

44
00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.390
derivative of a with respect to w is

45
00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:57.650
three.

46
00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.840
What is the derivative of the

47
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.480
loss with respect to w?

48
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:03.090
Six.

49
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:04.770
Two times three.

50
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.240
That one multiplication, repeated at

51
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.180
every node from the loss backwards,

52
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.560
is all backpropagation does.

53
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.260
In the app, open the backprop lab.

54
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.600
Run one forward pass, then step the

55
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.640
gradients backwards node by node and

56
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.700
check each multiplication.

57
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:26.790
Your turn.

58
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.280
Why does the gradient shrink as it travels

59
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.960
back through many sigmoid layers?

60
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.610
Because each local derivative is at

61
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.850
most a quarter, and they multiply.

62
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.710
Remember: backprop is the chain rule on a

63
00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.590
graph, one backward pass gives every

64
00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.230
gradient, and small locals vanish.

65
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.170
Try it in your next lecture: draw a

66
00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.490
three-node graph with real numbers on the

67
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:53.710
board.

68
00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.790
Pairs compute every gradient by hand, then

69
00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.430
check against the framework's answer.
